<div class="page">

  <header>
    <p class="eyebrow">CLI release engineering &middot; investigation</p>
    <h1>Releasing Without GitHub</h1>
    <p class="lede">Every way a user gets the Entire CLI today runs through github.com &mdash; the installer script,
      the version lookup, and the bytes themselves. One path is already immune, and roughly half our install
      traffic can be moved off GitHub with a Terraform change and eleven lines of GoReleaser config.</p>
    <p class="byline">Investigated 2026-08-18 &middot; entireio/cli @ soph/github-independent-release &middot; download counts from the GitHub releases API</p>
  </header>

  <section>
    <div class="stats">
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="chip-n">54%</span>
        <p>of v0.10.0 installs came through <span class="mono">install.sh</span> &mdash; the one path we fully control end to end.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="chip-n">4</span>
        <p>separate GitHub hops in a single <span class="mono">curl … | bash</span>: script, version, archive, checksums.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="chip-n">1</span>
        <p>install path already survives a GitHub outage: <span class="mono">go install</span>, served from the module proxy.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="stat">
        <span class="chip-n">35&nbsp;GB</span>
        <p>egress per stable release (v0.10.0; v0.9.0 was 90&nbsp;GB) &mdash; small enough that mirroring is a rounding error.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>The short answer</h2>
    <p>Split the problem in two. <em>Serving</em> a release without GitHub is cheap and worth doing now.
      <em>Cutting</em> a new release without GitHub is a real project, and the pieces we would need already
      exist elsewhere in the org.</p>
    <p>The single worst dependency is the one nobody would guess: <code>https://entire.io/install.sh</code> is a
      Cloudflare 307 redirect to <code>raw.githubusercontent.com</code>. The install command printed on the
      marketing site, in the README, and by the CLI's own update prompt fails at hop zero when GitHub is down.
      It is also a latent correctness bug &mdash; the redirect targets
      <code>refs/heads/main</code>, so users always run <em>main's</em> installer, never the released one.</p>
  </section>

