  <section>
    <h2>What to change, in order</h2>

    <div class="phase">
      <span class="n">1</span>
      <div class="body">
        <h3>Stop redirecting install.sh to GitHub</h3>
        <p class="cost">Hours &middot; one Terraform change &middot; no CLI release needed</p>
        <p>The rule lives in <code class="mono">infra/cloudflare/entire.io/redirect_rules.tf</code> as a
          zone-level dynamic redirect, so it fires before the marketing Worker's routes ever see the request.
          Serve the script from Cloudflare instead &mdash; either add <span class="mono">/install.sh</span> to the
          Worker's route list and ship the script as an asset, or point the rule at an R2/Pages object we
          publish at release time.</p>
        <p>Do the same for the <span class="mono">partial.to</span> twin, which carries a byte-identical rule.
          Then fix the version skew while we're there: publish the <em>released</em> installer, not
          <span class="mono">main</span>'s.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="phase">
      <span class="n">2</span>
      <div class="body">
        <h3>Publish every release to a second origin, and read from it first</h3>
        <p class="cost">Days &middot; Terraform + GoReleaser + install.sh + versioncheck</p>
        <p>Bring back <span class="mono">dl.entire.io</span> (it existed until commit
          <span class="mono">5e529c01</span> removed it) in front of a public release bucket, and add a
          <code>blobs:</code> block to <code>.goreleaser.yaml</code> writing
          <span class="mono">v{{.Version}}/</span>. The nonprod
          <span class="mono">git-remote-entire</span> publisher already does exactly this, so the pattern,
          the module, the OIDC wiring and the Security Hub exception are all precedented.</p>
        <p>Then move the readers over, GitHub demoted to fallback:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Version discovery</strong> &mdash; write <span class="mono">channels/stable.json</span> and
            <span class="mono">channels/nightly.json</span> at release time and read those instead of
            <span class="mono">api.github.com</span>, in both <span class="mono">install.sh</span> and
            <code>versioncheck</code>. This also retires an unauthenticated 60-request/hour/IP limit that can
            already break update checks behind a shared office or CI NAT.</li>
          <li><strong>Cask and Scoop download URLs</strong> &mdash; <code>homebrew_casks[].url.template</code> and
            <code>scoops[].url_template</code> both exist in the GoReleaser schema. Pointing them at
            <span class="mono">dl.entire.io</span> moves the other ~46% of bytes off GitHub with
            <em>no</em> loss of integrity: the sha256 is pinned in the manifest, which still comes from the tap.</li>
          <li><strong>install.sh ergonomics</strong> &mdash; add <span class="mono">--version</span> for pinning and
            an <span class="mono">ENTIRE_INSTALL_BASE_URL</span> override, so a human debugging an outage has
            something to reach for.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Make the new origin <em>primary</em> rather than a standby. A fallback that never runs is a fallback
          that is broken when it matters, and at ~35&nbsp;GB per stable release the egress is negligible either
          way.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="phase">
      <span class="n">3</span>
      <div class="body">
        <h3>Write the break-glass runbook &mdash; then decide whether to automate it</h3>
        <p class="cost">A week for a rehearsed manual path &middot; longer to automate</p>
        <p>There is no release runbook today. Cutting a release with GitHub down needs four things, and three
          of them already exist somewhere in the org:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Source</strong> &mdash; a non-GitHub mirror of the repo. We ship
            <span class="mono">entire repo mirror</span>; entiredb already clones over
            <span class="mono">entire://</span> in CI.</li>
          <li><strong>Compute</strong> &mdash; Buildkite Cloud, whose entiredb pipeline is explicitly built so
            that "there is no GitHub App and no GitHub in this loop". Linux-only is fine: GoReleaser signs and
            notarizes macOS binaries from Linux today.</li>
          <li><strong>A build image</strong> &mdash; the gap. Bootstrapping pulls goreleaser-pro and the mise
            tools from GitHub release tarballs, so a break-glass build cannot even start without a pre-baked
            image in a non-GitHub registry.</li>
          <li><strong>Secrets</strong> &mdash; the macOS signing cert, the notary key, the GoReleaser licence and
            the PostHog key exist only as GitHub Actions secrets. Mirror them to 1Password or AWS Secrets
            Manager or the break-glass build produces binaries Gatekeeper kills.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>The runbook should also document the escape hatch we already have and never mention:
          <code>go install github.com/entireio/cli/cmd/entire@v0.10.0</code> works during a GitHub outage,
          because <span class="mono">proxy.golang.org</span> serves cached module content and
          <code>versioninfo</code> recovers the real version from build info. Pin the version rather than
          using <span class="mono">@latest</span>, and add a release step that warms the proxy so a new
          version is cached <em>before</em> the next outage. The caveats are honest ones: no telemetry key, and
          experimental commands stay visible.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Blockers already in our own infra</h2>
    <p class="prose">Three things would stop phase&nbsp;2 on the first try if we assumed the existing bucket
      just works.</p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><strong>The upload role cannot be assumed by a release.</strong>
        <span class="mono">github-actions-public-release-upload</span> trusts
        <span class="mono">sub = repo:entireio/cli:ref:refs/heads/main</span> as an exact match. A release runs
        on a tag, so its OIDC subject is <span class="mono">refs/tags/vX.Y.Z</span> and
        <span class="mono">AssumeRoleWithWebIdentity</span> is refused. Needs a tag-aware condition or a second role.</li>
      <li><strong>Anonymous reads are limited to <span class="mono">commit/*</span>.</strong>
        Artifacts under <span class="mono">v0.10.0/</span> would upload fine and then 403 for every user.
        The bucket policy needs the new prefix.</li>
      <li><strong>That bucket is the wrong home for customer downloads.</strong> It lives in the
        <span class="mono">entire-nonprod</span> account with versioning off, no lifecycle rules and
        <span class="mono">enforce_tls = false</span>. Reuse the pattern; provision a prod bucket (or an R2
        bucket in the Cloudflare account already fronting entire.io) rather than the bucket itself.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="note">
      <strong>One thing to decide deliberately:</strong> nothing we ship is signed except the macOS
      notarization &mdash; no cosign, no SBOM, no attestations. Today <span class="mono">install.sh</span> trusts
      <span class="mono">checksums.txt</span> from the same origin as the archive, so a second origin doubles the
      number of hosts that can serve a bad binary. The cheap fix needs no key management: fetch the ~1&nbsp;KB
      <span class="mono">checksums.txt</span> from both origins and require them to agree, degrading to one
      origin when the other is down. Signing <span class="mono">checksums.txt</span> with a key pinned in the
      installer and the CLI is the stronger answer if we want it.
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="prose">
    <h2>Open questions</h2>
